# The SIGIL toolchain sigil-pi is built and tested against.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# sigil-pi is not self-contained: every tool is forged by a sigil-mcp/sigil-serve
# binary built from a sibling SIGIL checkout ($SIGIL_ROOT, default ../SIGIL) —
# public since 2026-09-04: https://github.com/nxrobins/sigil.
# Nothing used to record WHICH toolchain. On 2026-07-31 a rebuild of that sibling
# turned the whole suite red (44 failures) with no change to sigil-pi at all, and
# there was no way to tell what had moved. Recording it — and checking it in
# ci.sh — turns a baffling failure into a one-line diagnosis.
#
# WHY TREE HASHES, NOT A COMMIT SHA
# SIGIL commits to bench/, docs/ and rl_tasks/ constantly; none of that can change
# the forge binary. A commit pin trips on every one of them (it did, within three
# minutes of being written) and would train everyone to ignore it. These are the
# git tree hashes of the only two directories the binary is built from, so the
# check fires if and only if the toolchain itself actually moved.
#   git -C $SIGIL_ROOT rev-parse HEAD:crates HEAD:stdlib
#
# FORMAT: one `key = value` per line. Blank lines and `#` comments ignored.

crates = 5ffb00735509f9f871ddd11e3664db265b320ac0
stdlib = 3690c875f8bb86a2cafae35486c187429bd9c5fc

# THE SECOND PIN MODE: sha256_<platform>
#
# The tree hashes above can only be checked by someone who can CLONE SIGIL.
# While SIGIL was private that was the reason sigil-pi could not be installed
# by anyone outside it (issue #15); its source has been public since
# 2026-09-04, so anyone can. An INSTALLED toolchain still has no tree to
# hash, so it is pinned by the digest of the binary instead:
#
#   sha256_linux_x86_64 = <64 hex chars>
#   sha256_macos_arm64  = <64 hex chars>
#
# Platform keys are `<system>_<machine>` as toolchain.platform_key() computes
# them (x86_64/amd64 and arm64/aarch64 normalised to one spelling each), one
# line per published platform — a single digest cannot describe two builds,
# and a pin that silently matched the wrong platform would be worse than none.
#
# WHICH MODE APPLIES is decided by what actually resolved, never assumed:
# ci.sh checks the tree hashes because it rebuilds from source, and `main()`
# checks the sha256 because it runs whatever was installed. An ABSENT key is
# not a failure — there are no published binaries yet, and treating "nothing
# to check" as tampering would make the first release unrunnable. It does mean
# an installed toolchain is currently UNVERIFIED, which is the honest state
# and changes the day SIGIL publishes BINARIES — its source is public, its
# releases carry no toolchain binaries yet.

# SOLVER-VERIFYING BUILDS ONLY. ci.sh step 1 and scripts/build_release.py build
# sigil-mcp/sigil-serve with `--features sigil-mcp/solver,sigil-serve/solver`.
# The default (solver-off) build skips the Z3 proofs, and its forge gate fails
# closed (R817) unless SIGIL_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_CERT=1 — an override SIGIL's bench
# harness sets and this host's vendored client strips. Found 2026-08-22: the
# merged suite went red on every forge until the gate built what the product
# actually runs. Needs a Z3 with headers (Z3_SYS_Z3_HEADER + LIBRARY_PATH; a
# Homebrew z3 is found automatically); CI pins the official 4.12.2 release that
# SIGIL's own solver lane pins. The binary links libz3 dynamically — a host
# running it must provide that library.

# Human-readable note shown when the check fails.
note = public nxrobins/sigil main, "Sync from private 0a21a83a" (2026-09-05); rustc 1.98.0 via its rust-toolchain.toml

# A commit that CONTAINS the trees above. Convenience only — CI checks this out
# as a candidate and the tree hashes above remain the authority. Local commits
# are fine for local use, but CI can only fetch what is PUSHED.
#
# MUST BE THE FULL 40-CHAR SHA. actions/checkout only treats a value as a
# commit at full length; anything shorter it reads as a branch/tag name,
# fetches refs/heads/<ref>*, matches nothing, and fails with "The process
# '/usr/bin/git' failed with exit code 1" — naming neither the ref nor the
# cause. An abbreviated ref sat here through five PRs and was invisible until
# the forge job first actually ran (2026-08-02). Guarded now, both in
# tests/test_guards.py and the standalone CI job.
#
# PUSHED, AND PUBLIC: 8277a1d9 is the tip of main in github.com/nxrobins/sigil,
# the tree SIGIL exports from its private development ("Sync from private
# 0a21a83a", 2026-09-05). CI fetches it anonymously. It must stay reachable
# THERE: a ref that exists only in the private sigil-dev is a pin CI cannot
# follow, which is exactly what happened when SIGIL went public —
#
# THE NAME COLLISION THAT BROKE THE GATE SILENTLY. GitHub repository names are
# case-insensitive. The workflows checked out the private repo under its old
# name, `nxrobins/S...L` in capitals; it was renamed sigil-dev and the public
# export took the lowercase name. From that moment the old spelling resolved
# to the PUBLIC repo, where eb9f1715 did not exist, and the next forge run
# would have died inside actions/checkout naming neither the ref nor the
# cause. Found 2026-09-06, before a push hit it. The workflows now spell the
# repo the way GitHub does, and the forge job checks the ref is reachable in
# the public repo BEFORE checking it out, so this failure has a name.
#
# THE `forge` JOB RUNS, AND IS MANDATORY. It needed a `SIGIL_REPO_TOKEN`
# secret while SIGIL was private (first ran 2026-08-02 — which is how the
# abbreviated `ref` above was caught — and ran the full `./ci.sh` on every
# push since). It needs no secret now, and keeps the property that mattered:
# no job-level gate, so nothing about the toolchain's availability can turn
# the release gate into a skipped-but-satisfied check (a step-level gate
# once reported GREEN in ~7s while running nothing, PR #7; branch protection
# counts a skipped required check as satisfied). Check the PR checks, not
# this comment, for what actually ran.
ref = 8277a1d92d599df89e6b4391fc70fd0fa534d696

# RESOLVED AT THIS PIN (8277a1d9), found the first time the gate ran against it:
#   every tool — chat_turn under sigil-serve, every forge through the vendored
#   client — was refused with I013 ("formal CSIR verification failed"), a
#   compiler-INTEGRITY code, with no change to any .sigil file. Cause, from the
#   toolchain's own notes (docs/formal/public-occurrence-implementation.md,
#   2026-09-02): the CSIR v9 production verifier declares a host operation's
#   occurrence Public unless the host declares a profile, so a host call inside
#   a branch on an @Internal value — the previous call's error code, the shape
#   of every tool here — is refused as leaking Internal control (occurrence
#   detail 40). Not a policy the tools violate; the absence of a declaration.
#   The fix is the declaration: runtime_client.HOST_PROFILE names `ephemeral`
#   on every forge, and every sigil-serve config carries
#   `"host_profile": "ephemeral"`. Verified 2026-09-06 through the vendored
#   client: chat_turn, agent_turn+http/kv and parse_reply+json (the composed
#   stdlib json, loops included) all verify under the profile. A guard pins
#   the declaration in each place it must appear.
#
# RESOLVED AT eb9f1715, THE PREVIOUS PIN (kept: it is why pinning back to a
# pre-2026-07-21 toolchain is not an option to consider):
#   The `json` stdlib declared its parameters with no taint annotation, so they
#   defaulted to @Public and the tightened checker (f7fefd8f, 2026-07-21) rejected
#   the @Internal HTTP-response data parse_reply.sigil and chat_turn.sigil feed
#   them — 44 failures with no change to sigil-pi at all.
#
#   Fixed upstream in 801d0e1d, the way the note here asked for: `json::` is now
#   taint-POLYMORPHIC via a new `@Flow` annotation — the output label follows the
#   input label, so parsing @Internal bytes yields @Internal bytes and parsing
#   @Secret bytes yields @Secret bytes. Soundness is per-instantiation: each body
#   is checked once per admissible label, so a leak fails at the @Internal
#   instantiation. `kv::` and `http::`, whose returns were already @Internal, got
#   the matching @Internal on their parameters.
#
#   No declassify() was added anywhere in sigil-pi. parse_reply remains the
#   zero-grant inner-ring component with no downgrade in its data path — the
#   constraint the previous note set, met rather than traded away.
#
#   Pinning BACK to a pre-2026-07-21 toolchain is therefore no longer a live
#   option to consider: it is now strictly a downgrade, giving up both the
#   taint-soundness fixes that closed fail-open T001 holes (16f93fd2, bc1cfe25,
#   1eb5d516) and this fix, for nothing.
#
# NOTE: tools/chat_turn.sigil INLINES stdlib http + kv at generation time
# (make_chat_turn.py -> compose_with_stdlib). Moving the pinned stdlib/ tree
# therefore requires regenerating it, or ci.sh step 1 fails as stale.
